 Reflection on Microconrollers

In this lab we used the arduino nano esp32 in combination with chatgpt to design a variety of circuits. The first component we used was the potentiometer which gives a varying resistance based off where the slider inside is on a metal track. Turing the potentiometer changes its resistance altering the current or voltage passing through it. We used this to build a small circuit on a breadboard with an led and a resistor. Once connected to ground and power, turning the potentiometer changes the brightness of the LED. More resistance equals a dimmer LED. We had this circuit hooked up to a power supply and we then increased the voltage from 5v to 20v and turned the resistance on the potentiometer all the way down. This caused the resistor to pop and smoke as it was not built for this voltage and failed. From this we got a basic understanding of power from the formula P=I^2R (power, current, and resistance), and its effect on electrical components. We then swapped the potentiometer for a button allowing us to turn on and off the LED instead of dimming it. Then we put a light sensor in place of the button which changed the brightness of the LED based off of the surrounding light levels. Finally we put all our knowledge together to recreate a security circuit that had been mentioned previously which, if armed and if an intruder was detected, would trigger a flashing LED. We had to slightly modify the system for the provided materials. We wired our button sensor and led to our arduino and connected them to power. After we finished wiring the circuit we used chatgpt to write code that would complete the task with our layout. I learned that we need to know which pin layout we are using (GPIO) as well as the language (micropython) and system (arduino esp32) or our code would not work. We initially had trouble with the arming portion of the code as the LED would turn on if the system was armed and it would also turn on if the system wasn't armed and detected an intruder. It was frustrating trying to fix a problem with a low level understanding of the wiring and code. After talking with my lab partners and some trail and error we had on to do wiring fix and move one line of code, the circuit was a success. I learned that having the code print something for each portion of the code can be very useful to diagnosing problems. In this example we had the system print “armed” when the system is armed and “intruder detected” when the sensor picks something up. This made it much easier to fix our mistakes. I then added this code to my github portfolio and updated my Readme to indicate my progress. 

 After getting a basic understanding of how to wire and code a servo with arduino we began to construct a circuit and code that would move the servo 180 degrees when a button was pressed. We then constructed a circuit that would take readings from a rain drop sensor and plugged it into a multi meter where it would display the analog readings. We then recorded 3 readings for each condition of the sensor. We did it with the senor dry, with humidity from our breath, with a wet rag wiped on half, wiped on the whole thing, then pressed into the sensor. We then hooked the sensor up to an arduino and used chatgpt to write code that would convert the ADC values into a voltage and save it in a certain kind of file called a CSV that stores the data in a usable way. We then again added the code to our portfolio and updated our readme similarly. From this, I got a better understanding of what an analog signal is compared to a digital signal outside of just the concept. I also got a better understanding of different ways of storing and collecting data. We put our data from both types into google sheets and calculated the average and standard deviation for each. We also analyzed the experiment's effectiveness and pointed out flaws. From this module as a whole, I developed a better understanding of building circuits, troubleshooting code and wiring, and collecting and analyzing data. 

